that counts. “You’ve really got to use your grapevine, people you know who are in interesting jobs, people you’ve studied with – do they have any friends or parents in jobs that you’d like to do?” Dr Peter Hawkins, a graduate employment expert at Liverpool University said.","The problem is partly that there are not enough openings to go round. The expansion of higher education has not led to an increase in the demand for ‘knowledge workers’. Up to 40% of graduates are in non-graduate work.","Even work experience opportunities that sound exciting can prove to be deeply disappointing. There is, of course, a fine line between work experience and ‘slave labour’. One 23-year-old graduate, who wished to remain anonymous, said last week that a three- month stint with a television production company had put him off a broadcasting career.","“It taught me that in television there is an attitude that work experience people are free labour,” he said. “We were expected to do the dirty work for nothing.\"","However, even that sort of experience can be valuable. Dr Martin Stephen, headmaster of St Paul’s boys’ school, suggested that working in a supermarket should not be sniffed at.","“The work experience that I would personally support would be stacking shelves – I think it’s a wonderful experience,” he said. “It’s the real world. And I think it’s not a bad thing to make a child live off what they earn for five or six weeks. That’s real learning.”"],"options":[{"key":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"key":"B","text":"FALSE"},{"key":"C","text":"NOT ENOUGH INFORMATION"}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"stem":"It is common for parents to worry about their children’s education.","answer":"A"},{"n":22,"stem":"These days they also worry about whether their children can find the right kind of work experience.","answer":"A"},{"n":23,"stem":"Children from good schools are usually successful in finding good holiday jobs.","answer":"C"},{"n":24,"stem":"It is difficult for employers to find candidates with good academic results.","answer":"B"},{"n":25,"stem":"Employers want people who can work with others.","answer":"A"},{"n":26,"stem":"The idea that equality in education is a basic human right has become an election issue.","answer":"C"},{"n":27,"stem":"Allen & Overy say they don’t care if a candidate’s grades are good or not.","answer":"B"},{"n":28,"stem":"There are great regional differences in the availability of good jobs round the country.","answer":"C"},{"n":29,"stem":"Glamorous-sounding jobs can sometimes prove unsatisfactory.","answer":"A"},{"n":30,"stem":"Dr Martin Stephen believes young people should not be allowed to work in supermarkets.","answer":"B"}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2006-febr-emelt-L1$mtr$, $mtr$2006-febr-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 1, $mtr$choice$mtr$, $mtr$feleletválasztós szövegkiegészítés (A-D)$mtr$, 8, 5, $mtr${"id":"2006-febr-emelt-L1","number":1,"kind":"choice","layout":"inline","examType":"feleletválasztós szövegkiegészítés (A-D)","points":8,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about the intelligence of mammals. Some words are missing from the text.","Choose the most appropriate answer from the options (A-D) for each gap (1-8) in the text."],"title":"WHY CAN MAMMALS DEVELOP SUCH INTELLIGENCE?","body":["Compared to their body size, mammals have the largest brains of any group of animals. It is the brain that interprets {{0}} information coming in from the animals' senses and co- ordinates the response. Why do mammals need brains {{1}} other animals?","One reason is linked to the fact that they regulate their own body temperature. This is an expensive process, {{2}} lots of energy from food, and so mammals have to be very {{3}} in the way that they find food and extract all the nutrition they can from it. {{4}} reptiles have a rather simple approach to food: eat first, think later. Think of the way a crocodile behaves - it will lunge at anything {{5}} moves and therefore might be edible, and it takes in a lot of mud and other inedible things with its meal.","Mammals, {{6}}, can't afford to fill up on things that aren't going to {{7}} them any benefit. Instead, they are generally very selective about what they eat and make judgements about whether it is {{8}} it or not. This means that they may have similar senses to other animals, but they have a bigger brain to process what comes in, and judge what action to take."],"items":[{"n":0,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"all of"},{"key":"B","text":"all the"},{"key":"C","text":"whole"},{"key":"D","text":"pieces"}],"example":true,"answer":"B"},{"n":1,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"large as"},{"key":"B","text":"so large"},{"key":"C","text":"larger than"},{"key":"D","text":"as large as"}]},{"n":2,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"in need"},{"key":"B","text":"needing"},{"key":"C","text":"needed"},{"key":"D","text":"which need"}]},{"n":3,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"inefficient"},{"key":"B","text":"efficient"},{"key":"C","text":"ineffective"},{"key":"D","text":"effective"}]},{"n":4,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"Most"},{"key":"B","text":"Most of"},{"key":"C","text":"The most"},{"key":"D","text":"The most of"}]},{"n":5,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"that"},{"key":"B","text":"what"},{"key":"C","text":"but"},{"key":"D","text":"who"}]},{"n":6,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"despite"},{"key":"B","text":"but"},{"key":"C","text":"although"},{"key":"D","text":"though"}]},{"n":7,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"make"},{"key":"B","text":"take"},{"key":"C","text":"give"},{"key":"D","text":"do"}]},{"n":8,"options":[{"key":"A","text":"worthy"},{"key":"B","text":"worthless"},{"key":"C","text":"worse"},{"key":"D","text":"worth"}]}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
