"n":15,"accept":["were"]},{"n":16,"accept":["tell"]},{"n":17,"accept":["grew"]},{"n":18,"accept":["other"]},{"n":19,"accept":["not"]}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2007-okt-emelt-L3$mtr$, $mtr$2007-okt-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 3, $mtr$text$mtr$, $mtr$szóképzés (zárójeles szó)$mtr$, 10, 7, $mtr${"id":"2007-okt-emelt-L3","number":3,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"szóképzés (zárójeles szó)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about research on humour. Some words are missing from the text.","Use the words in brackets to form the words that fit in the gaps (20-29).","There might be cases when you do not have to change the word in brackets.","Use only one word for each gap."],"title":"THE CENTRE OF FUN","body":["Finding the centre of fun Decoding the brain has proved no {{0}} matter. People who have suffered {{20}} to the front of their brain do not get complex {{21}} stories or more sophisticated jokes, according to research.","The scientists from the University of Toronto say that, for the first time, they have {{22}} the main centre of humour - in the right frontal lobe of the brain. \"We've always thought of humour as an intangible part of our {{23}},\" said Dr Prathiba Shammi of the university's Department of Psychology.","The researchers tested numerous {{24}} to jokes in 31 adults aged 18 to 70. The study found that people with problems in the frontal lobe of their brain found it most difficult to appreciate {{25}} and verbal jokes and cartoons. They chose the wrong punchlines to jokes and were likely to choose totally {{26}} endings.","For example, they were asked to finish the {{27}} joke: A teenager is being interviewed for a summer job. \"You'll get $50 a week to start off,\" says his boss. \"Then after a month you'll get a {{28}} to $75 a week.\" The guinea pigs then had to choose from a range of punchlines: \"I'd like to take the job. When can I start?\" \"Hey boss, your nose is too big for your face!\" \"That's great! I'll come back in a month.\" The {{29}} answer was the correct one, but people with damage to their right frontal lobe tended to choose the second punchline."],"grading":{"spelling":"strict"},"items":[{"n":0,"prompt":"laugh","example":true,"accept":["laughing"]},{"n":20,"prompt":"injure"},{"n":21,"prompt":"humour"},{"n":22,"prompt":"local"},{"n":23,"prompt":"personal"},{"n":24,"prompt":"respond"},{"n":25,"prompt":"write"},{"n":26,"prompt":"logical"},{"n":27,"prompt":"follow"},{"n":28,"prompt":"rise"},{"n":29,"prompt":"three"}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2007-okt-emelt-L3$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2007-okt-emelt-L3","number":3,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"szóképzés (zárójeles szó)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about research on humour. Some words are missing from the text.","Use the words in brackets to form the words that fit in the gaps (20-29).","There might be cases when you do not have to change the word in brackets.","Use only one word for each gap."],"title":"THE CENTRE OF FUN","body":["Finding the centre of fun Decoding the brain has proved no {{0}} matter. People who have suffered {{20}} to the front of their brain do not get complex {{21}} stories or more sophisticated jokes, according to research.","The scientists from the University of Toronto say that, for the first time, they have {{22}} the main centre of humour - in the right frontal lobe of the brain. \"We've always thought of humour as an intangible part of our {{23}},\" said Dr Prathiba Shammi of the university's Department of Psychology.","The researchers tested numerous {{24}} to jokes in 31 adults aged 18 to 70. The study found that people with problems in the frontal lobe of their brain found it most difficult to appreciate {{25}} and verbal jokes and cartoons. They chose the wrong punchlines to jokes and were likely to choose totally {{26}} endings.","For example, they were asked to finish the {{27}} joke: A teenager is being interviewed for a summer job. \"You'll get $50 a week to start off,\" says his boss. \"Then after a month you'll get a {{28}} to $75 a week.\" The guinea pigs then had to choose from a range of punchlines: \"I'd like to take the job. When can I start?\" \"Hey boss, your nose is too big for your face!\" \"That's great! I'll come back in a month.\" The {{29}} answer was the correct one, but people with damage to their right frontal lobe tended to choose the second punchline."],"grading":{"spelling":"strict","maxWords":1},"items":[{"n":0,"prompt":"laugh","example":true,"accept":["laughing"]},{"n":20,"prompt":"injure","accept":["injuries","injury"]},{"n":21,"prompt":"humour","accept":["humorous"]},{"n":22,"prompt":"local","accept":["located"]},{"n":23,"prompt":"personal","accept":["personality"]},{"n":24,"prompt":"respond","accept":["responses"]},{"n":25,"prompt":"write","accept":["written"]},{"n":26,"prompt":"logical","accept":["illogical"]},{"n":27,"prompt":"follow","accept":["following"]},{"n":28,"prompt":"rise","accept":["rise"]},{"n":29,"prompt":"three","accept":["third"]}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2007-okt-emelt-L4$mtr$, $mtr$2007-okt-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 4, $mtr$lines$mtr$, $mtr$soronkénti hibakeresés (felesleges szó vagy helyes sor)$mtr$, 11, 8, $mtr${"id":"2007-okt-emelt-L4","number":4,"kind":"lines","layout":"lines","examType":"soronkénti hibakeresés (felesleges szó vagy helyes sor)","points":11,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about pigs. In most lines there is one word that should not be there. It is either grammatically incorrect or does not fit in with the sense of the text.","Read the text and then copy the extra word in the space provided after each line.","Some lines are correct. Indicate these lines with a tick (9).","The task begins with two examples (0)."],"title":"PIG FRIENDSHIP","intro":"","items":[{"n":"0a","example":true,"text":"I had a close friend who grew up on a farm in Indiana. We have","answer":"have"},{"n":"0b","example":true,"text":"used to share stories of our childhood. Since my Mom was English,","answer":null},{"n":30,"text":"I was forced to eat \"fish and chips\" several times a week. Given"},{"n":31,"text":"the choice, I would rather have been enjoyed some BBQ ribs. Rob,"},{"n":32,"text":"on the other hand, loved fish and fishing, but absolutely refused to"},{"n":33,"text":"eat pork. I once asked him why he has disliked pork. Having lived on"},{"n":34,"text":"a farm, I expected he would tell me how much dirty they were. “Pigs"},{"n":35,"text":"are emotionally very much would like humans,” Rob said, “and once"},{"n":36,"text":"you’ve not been around them you just can’t eat them.” Rob told me a"},{"n":37,"text":"collection of farm stories, mostly dealing with pigs’ friendly character."},{"n":38,"text":"I was reminded of these pig stories when I had heard that a lot of"},{"n":39,"text":"households in America had beaten potbellied pigs as indoor pets."},{"n":40,"text":"Their keen on intellect and strong emotional bond made them “family"}],"outro":"members” to the lonely and elderly."}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
