 an interest in a domain, while the third, go-live phase – which started on 17 July 2008 - opened up the registry to customers worldwide. On the first day of the go-live period, we had 50,000 registrations.\"","One buyer of the .me domain was Matt Mansell, who purchased willshemarry.me. As it turns out she did marry him – and the site was used as a way of informing guests how to get to the wedding. But, aside from his new wife, the domain name may prove to be Mr Mansell's greatest gain from his wedding. \"I've had an awful lot of people who want to buy the idea; I've had people who want to buy the domain. A lot of the .me names are actually selling at online auctions for figures like $10,000-$15,000.\"","Although it is a very lucrative market, technology commentator Bill Thompson is not convinced with the value attached to a memorable domain name. \"I'm a domain name cynic. More and more people just go to their favourite search engine, type in what they're looking for and don't actually look for where it's going. So I just don't think domain names are as important as they were. And I don't think they should be.\"","(http://news.bbc.co.uk)"],"options":[{"key":"A","text":"TRUE"},{"key":"B","text":"FALSE"},{"key":"C","text":"NOT ENOUGH INFORMATION"}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"stem":"The domain name of Tuvalu appeals to television stations.","answer":"A"},{"n":24,"stem":"After 2006 Montenegro managed to hold on to its original domain name.","answer":"B"},{"n":25,"stem":"Montenegro domain names can function as a simple request.","answer":"A"},{"n":26,"stem":"The .me domain is less popular in other languages than English.","answer":"B"},{"n":27,"stem":"Before 17 July 2008 only local domain registrations were accepted.","answer":"B"},{"n":28,"stem":"Many of the people registering in Montenegro intended to go and live there.","answer":"C"},{"n":29,"stem":"Mr Mansell says that .me domains can be bought for $10,000 to $15,000 at the Montenegro registry office.","answer":"B"},{"n":30,"stem":"Mr Thompson is not enthusiastic about the domain business even though it is highly profitable.","answer":"A"}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2011-maj-emelt-L1$mtr$, $mtr$2011-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 1, $mtr$text$mtr$, $mtr$szóképzés (zárójeles szó)$mtr$, 10, 5, $mtr${"id":"2011-maj-emelt-L1","number":1,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"szóképzés (zárójeles szó)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read a part of a travel brochure advertising Ireland. Some words are missing from the text.","Use the words in brackets to form the words that fit in the gaps (1-10).","There might be cases when you do not have to change the word in brackets.","Use only one word for each gap."],"title":"VISIT IRELAND!","body":["Irish culture is rich in its {{0}}. A holiday in Ireland allows one to experience a culture deeply steeped in history. This history is reflected in the warm {{1}}, underrated delicious traditional dishes, brilliant music, and beautiful landscape.","Whether enjoying breakfast at a B&B, downing a pint or two in a pub, experiencing the local artists, or hiking along a seaside cliff, it is hard not to feel a part of the {{2}} culture and rich history that is Ireland. Understanding the history of Ireland ensures a deeper {{3}} of the ancient monuments found there today.","The reputation of the \"Friendly Irish\" is not a myth. You will nearly always find a hand outstretched with the {{4}} \"how are you?\"","Without any doubt pubs are the main places for {{5}} in Ireland. You can drink, eat, dance there, join in a sing-song or listen to a group of {{6}}. Food in pubs, known as \"pub grub\" is generally good and the prices are {{7}}.","Although Gaelic and English are both the {{8}} languages of Ireland less than 5% of the population use Gaelic on a {{9}} basis. In Irish schools Gaelic is {{10}} and currently there is a renewed interest in the language.","(www.travelchannel.co.uk)"],"grading":{"spelling":"strict"},"items":[{"n":0,"prompt":"diverse","example":true,"accept":["diversity"]},{"n":1,"prompt":"hospitable"},{"n":2,"prompt":"drama"},{"n":3,"prompt":"appreciate"},{"n":4,"prompt":"greet"},{"n":5,"prompt":"social"},{"n":6,"prompt":"music"},{"n":7,"prompt":"reason"},{"n":8,"prompt":"office"},{"n":9,"prompt":"day"},{"n":10,"prompt":"obligation"}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2011-maj-emelt-L1$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2011-maj-emelt-L1","number":1,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"szóképzés (zárójeles szó)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read a part of a travel brochure advertising Ireland. Some words are missing from the text.","Use the words in brackets to form the words that fit in the gaps (1-10).","There might be cases when you do not have to change the word in brackets.","Use only one word for each gap."],"title":"VISIT IRELAND!","body":["Irish culture is rich in its {{0}}. A holiday in Ireland allows one to experience a culture deeply steeped in history. This history is reflected in the warm {{1}}, underrated delicious traditional dishes, brilliant music, and beautiful landscape.","Whether enjoying breakfast at a B&B, downing a pint or two in a pub, experiencing the local artists, or hiking along a seaside cliff, it is hard not to feel a part of the {{2}} culture and rich history that is Ireland. Understanding the history of Ireland ensures a deeper {{3}} of the ancient monuments found there today.","The reputation of the \"Friendly Irish\" is not a myth. You will nearly always find a hand outstretched with the {{4}} \"how are you?\"","Without any doubt pubs are the main places for {{5}} in Ireland. You can drink, eat, dance there, join in a sing-song or listen to a group of {{6}}. Food in pubs, known as \"pub grub\" is generally good and the prices are {{7}}.","Although Gaelic and English are both the {{8}} languages of Ireland less than 5% of the population use Gaelic on a {{9}} basis. In Irish schools Gaelic is {{10}} and currently there is a renewed interest in the language.","(www.travelchannel.co.uk)"],"grading":{"spelling":"strict","maxWords":1},"items":[{"n":0,"prompt":"diverse","example":true,"accept":["diversity"]},{"n":1,"prompt":"hospitable","accept":["hospitality"]},{"n":2,"prompt":"drama","accept":["dramatic"]},{"n":3,"prompt":"appreciate","accept":["appreciation"]},{"n":4,"prompt":"greet","accept":["greeting"]},{"n":5,"prompt":"social","accept":["socialising","socializing","society"]},{"n":6,"prompt":"music","accept":["musicians","music-makers"]},{"n":7,"prompt":"reason","accept":["reasonable"]},{"n":8,"prompt":"office","accept":["official"]},{"n":9,"prompt":"day","accept":["daily","day-to-day"]},{"n":10,"prompt":"obligation","accept":["obligatory"]}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2011-maj-emelt-L2$mtr$, $mtr$2011-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 2, $mtr$text$mtr$, $mtr$nyílt cloze (egy szó)$mtr$, 10, 6, $mtr${"id":"2011-maj-emelt-L2","number":2,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"nyílt cloze (egy szó)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read a passage from a book about the history of cars. Some words are missing from the text.","Your task is to write the missing words on the dotted lines (11-20) after the text.","Use only one word in each gap."],"title":"THE EARLY DAYS OF CARS","body":["The first motor cars owed a lot {{0}} the horse carriage. Indeed, many pioneering cars were simply horse carts with an engine – that is {{11}} they were known as horseless carriages. They were notoriously unreliable. Sometimes a couple of horses were taken along as well in {{12}} of a breakdown.","Early cars were hard to start and drive, and even harder to stop. Yet the roads were full of hazards: potholes, sharp bends, steep hills, and stray animals. Even the shortest outing in a car rarely passed {{13}} an incident. Road signs were put up to warn drivers of coming dangers, but unwary animals and pedestrians were too often hit by speeding motor cars. {{14}} protect people, horns and other warning devices were made compulsory.","Cars {{15}} been restricted by speed limits right from the start. In Britain, there was the \"Red Flag\" Act, {{16}} required that all cars have two drivers, while a third person walked in front waving a red flag. The world's first speeding ticket was issued in Great Britain in 1896. The driver was fined a shilling {{17}} doing 8 mph in a 2-mph zone.","Night driving caused problems too. Lighting was {{18}} poor that few motorists ventured out on the road after dark. The lights on the first cars were candle lamps inherited from horse-drawn carriages. They were very dim so they {{19}} little more than warn other road users of the car's presence. Although special car lights were soon developed, they were considered luxury accessories for many years. It was {{20}} until the 1930s that bright electric lights were fitted as standard on most cars.","(Eyewitness Books: Car)"],"grading":{"spelling":"strict"},"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"accept":["to"]},{"n":11},{"n":12},{"n":13},{"n":14},{"n":15},{"n":16},{"n":17},{"n":18},{"n":19},{"n":20}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2011-maj-emelt-L2$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2011-maj-emelt-L2","number":2,"kind":"text","layout":"inline","examType":"nyílt cloze (egy szó)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read a passage from a book about the history of cars. Some words are missing from the text.","Your task is to write the missing words on the dotted lines (11-20) after the text.","Use only one word in each gap."],"title":"THE EARLY DAYS OF CARS","body":["The first motor cars owed a lot {{0}} the horse carriage. Indeed, many pioneering cars were simply horse carts with an engine – that is {{11}} they were known as horseless carriages. They were notoriously unreliable. Sometimes a couple of horses were taken along as well in {{12}} of a breakdown.","Early cars were hard to start and drive, and even harder to stop. Yet the roads were full of hazards: potholes, sharp bends, steep hills, and stray animals. Even the shortest outing in a car rarely passed {{13}} an incident. Road signs were put up to warn drivers of coming dangers, but unwary animals and pedestrians were too often hit by speeding motor cars. {{14}} protect people, horns and other warning devices were made compulsory.","Cars {{15}} been restricted by speed limits right from the start. In Britain, there was the \"Red Flag\" Act, {{16}} required that all cars have two drivers, while a third person walked in front waving a red flag. The world's first speeding ticket was issued in Great Britain in 1896. The driver was fined a shilling {{17}} doing 8 mph in a 2-mph zone.","Night driving caused problems too. Lighting was {{18}} poor that few motorists ventured out on the road after dark. The lights on the first cars were candle lamps inherited from horse-drawn carriages. They were very dim so they {{19}} little more than warn other road users of the car's presence. Although special car lights were soon developed, they were considered luxury accessories for many years. It was {{20}} until the 1930s that bright electric lights were fitted as standard on most cars.","(Eyewitness Books: Car)"],"grading":{"spelling":"strict","maxWords":1},"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"accept":["to"]},{"n":11,"accept":["why"]},{"n":12,"accept":["case"]},{"n":13,"accept":["without"]},{"n":14,"accept":["To"]},{"n":15,"accept":["have","had"]},{"n":16,"accept":["which"]},{"n":17,"accept":["for","after"]},{"n":18,"accept":["so"]},{"n":19,"accept":["did"]},{"n":20,"accept":["not"]}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2011-maj-emelt-L3$mtr$, $mtr$2011-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 3, $mtr$bank$mtr$, $mtr$szóbank (szavak visszahelyezése)$mtr$, 10, 7, $mtr${"id":"2011-maj-emelt-L3","number":3,"kind":"bank","layout":"inline","examType":"szóbank (szavak visszahelyezése)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about a law case. Some words are missing from the text.","Your task is to choose the most appropriate word from the list (A-N) for each gap (21-30) in the text. Write the letter of the appropriate word in the white box.","There are two extra words that you do not need to use."],"title":"MACDONALD'S LOSES HAMBURGER CASE","body":["A McDonald's outlet in the Netherlands was {{0}} to fire an employee for giving a colleague a piece of cheese on a hamburger, a court has ruled.","The waitress was {{21}} 10 months ago after she sold a hamburger to a co-worker who {{22}} asked for cheese, which she added. The fast-food chain argued this {{23}} the hamburger into a cheeseburger, and so she should have {{24}} more. But the district court ruled a written warning {{25}} have been more appropriate.","McDonald's was ordered to pay the former employee more than €4,200 for the last five months of her {{26}}. The fast-food chain had argued that the waitress – who was employed at a branch in the northern town of Lemmer – had broken staff rules {{27}} free gifts to family, friends or colleagues. But the court {{28}} in its written judgement: \"The dismissal was {{29}} severe a measure. It is just a slice of cheese,\" reports AFP news agency.","The {{30}} comes days after McDonald's reported an increase in net profits by almost a quarter during the last three months.","(www.bbc.co.uk)"],"bank":[{"key":"A","text":"AFTER"},{"key":"B","text":"CHARGED"},{"key":"C","text":"WRONG"},{"key":"D","text":"CONTRACT"},{"key":"E","text":"MUST"},{"key":"F","text":"PROHIBITING"},{"key":"G","text":"RULING"},{"key":"H","text":"SACKED"},{"key":"I","text":"SAID"},{"key":"K","text":"THEN"},{"key":"L","text":"TOO"},{"key":"M","text":"TURNED"},{"key":"N","text":"WOULD"}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"answer":"C"},{"n":21},{"n":22},{"n":23},{"n":24},{"n":25},{"n":26},{"n":27},{"n":28},{"n":29},{"n":30}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
insert into public.task_keys (task_id, task) values ($mtr$2011-maj-emelt-L3$mtr$, $mtr${"id":"2011-maj-emelt-L3","number":3,"kind":"bank","layout":"inline","examType":"szóbank (szavak visszahelyezése)","points":10,"instructions":["You are going to read an article about a law case. Some words are missing from the text.","Your task is to choose the most appropriate word from the list (A-N) for each gap (21-30) in the text. Write the letter of the appropriate word in the white box.","There are two extra words that you do not need to use."],"title":"MACDONALD'S LOSES HAMBURGER CASE","body":["A McDonald's outlet in the Netherlands was {{0}} to fire an employee for giving a colleague a piece of cheese on a hamburger, a court has ruled.","The waitress was {{21}} 10 months ago after she sold a hamburger to a co-worker who {{22}} asked for cheese, which she added. The fast-food chain argued this {{23}} the hamburger into a cheeseburger, and so she should have {{24}} more. But the district court ruled a written warning {{25}} have been more appropriate.","McDonald's was ordered to pay the former employee more than €4,200 for the last five months of her {{26}}. The fast-food chain had argued that the waitress – who was employed at a branch in the northern town of Lemmer – had broken staff rules {{27}} free gifts to family, friends or colleagues. But the court {{28}} in its written judgement: \"The dismissal was {{29}} severe a measure. It is just a slice of cheese,\" reports AFP news agency.","The {{30}} comes days after McDonald's reported an increase in net profits by almost a quarter during the last three months.","(www.bbc.co.uk)"],"bank":[{"key":"A","text":"AFTER"},{"key":"B","text":"CHARGED"},{"key":"C","text":"WRONG"},{"key":"D","text":"CONTRACT"},{"key":"E","text":"MUST"},{"key":"F","text":"PROHIBITING"},{"key":"G","text":"RULING"},{"key":"H","text":"SACKED"},{"key":"I","text":"SAID"},{"key":"K","text":"THEN"},{"key":"L","text":"TOO"},{"key":"M","text":"TURNED"},{"key":"N","text":"WOULD"}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"answer":"C"},{"n":21,"answer":"H"},{"n":22,"answer":"K"},{"n":23,"answer":"M"},{"n":24,"answer":"B"},{"n":25,"answer":"N"},{"n":26,"answer":"D"},{"n":27,"answer":"F"},{"n":28,"answer":"I"},{"n":29,"answer":"L"},{"n":30,"answer":"G"}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2011-maj-emelt-L4$mtr$, $mtr$2011-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$language$mtr$, 4, $mtr$lines$mtr$, $mtr$soronkénti hibakeresés (felesleges szó vagy helyes sor)$mtr$, 14, 8, $mtr${"id":"2011-maj-emelt-L4","number":4,"kind":"lines","layout":"lines","examType":"soronkénti hibakeresés (felesleges szó vagy helyes sor)","points":14,"instructions":["You are going to read an anecdote about a U.S. inventor. In most lines there is one word that should not be there. It is either grammatically incorrect or does not fit in with the sense of the text.","Read the text and then copy the extra word in the space provided after each line.","Some lines are correct. Indicate these lines with a tick ().","The task begins with two examples (0)."],"title":"THE TURNSTILE","items":[{"n":"0a","example":true,"text":"Thomas Edison (1847-1931) is responsible for about 1300","answer":null},{"n":"0b","example":true,"text":"inventions, and the most famous of which is the electric light bulb.","answer":"and"},{"n":31,"text":"He has also invented the gramophone and motion pictures, and his"},{"n":32,"text":"carbon transmitter enabled Bell's telephone to be brought up into"},{"n":33,"text":"practical use. Edison had a great faith in progress and industry, and"},{"n":34,"text":"never valued very hard work. He used to say, \"Genius is 1%"},{"n":35,"text":"inspiration and 99% perspiration.\" Here is an anecdote about him:"},{"n":36,"text":"Edison had a summer residence of which he was very much"},{"n":37,"text":"proud. He enjoyed showing off visitors around his property,"},{"n":38,"text":"pointing out the various labour-saving devices. At one point it was"},{"n":39,"text":"necessary to pass through a turnstile in correct order to take the"},{"n":40,"text":"main path back to the house. Considerable effort was needed not to"},{"n":41,"text":"move the turnstile. A guest asked from Edison why it was that, with"},{"n":42,"text":"all the other clever gadgets around, he had been such a heavy"},{"n":43,"text":"turnstile. Edison replied him, \"Well, you see, everyone who pushes"},{"n":44,"text":"the turnstile around these pumps eight gallons of water into the tank"}],"outro":"on my roof.\" (Paw Prints Anecdotes)"}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
