 meadow in the spring and summer. Bring a picnic, not a disposable barbecue. This used to be one of the few open spaces in London where you could barbecue legally, but that’s been changed recently due to cleaning costs.","Green Park – This green, triangle-shaped area was enclosed by Charles II as a hunting ground in 1668. Thankfully, it was opened to the public in 1826 and today you’ll find lunching commuters and tourists lounging in the park’s famous stripy deckchairs.","Hyde Park is one of the largest and maybe the most famous of London’s Royal Parks with London’s oldest boating lake. Catch a glimpse of the Victorian pet cemetery hidden in the park’s north-west corner, where around 300 furry Londoners from the past rest in peace."],"bank":[{"key":"A","text":"is ideal for people who love various types of outdoor exercise."},{"key":"B","text":"is the place where Henry VIII used to hunt for deer."},{"key":"C","text":"has separate lakes for men and women to swim in."},{"key":"D","text":"has an area that used to be a burial ground for animals."},{"key":"E","text":"doesn’t allow visitors to have open fires while hanging out there."},{"key":"F","text":"was named after a building that does not exist anymore."},{"key":"G","text":"is home to various species of wild animals."},{"key":"H","text":"charges people for boating in its pond."},{"key":"I","text":"has a lot of sculptures of historic figures."},{"key":"K","text":"used to serve the hobby of a monarch."}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"stem":"Hampstead Heath ...","answer":"C"},{"n":15,"stem":"Crystal Palace Park ...","answer":"F"},{"n":16,"stem":"Dulwich Park ...","answer":"A"},{"n":17,"stem":"Greenwich Park ...","answer":"G"},{"n":18,"stem":"London Fields ...","answer":"E"},{"n":19,"stem":"Green Park ...","answer":"K"},{"n":20,"stem":"Hyde Park ...","answer":"D"}]}$mtr$::jsonb) on conflict (task_id) do update set task = excluded.task;
insert into public.tasks (id, exam_id, section, number, kind, exam_type, points, position, content, audio) values ($mtr$2024-maj-emelt-R4$mtr$, $mtr$2024-maj-emelt$mtr$, $mtr$reading$mtr$, 4, $mtr$bank$mtr$, $mtr$mondatrész visszahelyezése szövegbe$mtr$, 8, 4, $mtr${"id":"2024-maj-emelt-R4","number":4,"kind":"bank","layout":"inline","examType":"mondatrész visszahelyezése szövegbe","points":8,"instructions":["Read this article about the word of the year in 2023.","Some parts of sentences have been left out from the text.","Your task is to reconstruct the text by filling in the gaps (21-28) from the list (A-L) below.","Remember that there are two extra letters that you do not need."],"title":"CAMBRIDGE DICTIONARY’S WORD OF THE YEAR IN 2023","body":["Cambridge dictionary’s word of the year for 2023 is “hallucinate” – {{0}}.","The original definition of the chosen word is to “seem to see, hear, feel, or smell” something that does not exist, usually because of “a health condition or {{21}}”. It now has an additional meaning, relating to when artificial intelligence systems such as ChatGPT, which generates text that mimics human writing, “hallucinates” and {{22}}.","The word was chosen because the new meaning “gets to the heart of why people are talking about AI”, according to a post on the dictionary site. Generative AI is a “powerful” but “far from perfect” tool, “one we’re all still learning how {{23}} – this means being aware of both its potential strengths and its current weaknesses.”","“AI hallucinations remind us that humans still need {{24}} to the use of these tools,” continued the post. “Large language models (LLMs) are only as reliable as the information their algorithms learn from. Human expertise is arguably more important than ever, {{25}} that large language models can be trained on.”","Henry Shevlin, an AI ethicist at the University of Cambridge, said that it is striking that rather than choosing a computer-specific term like “bugs” to describe the mistakes that LLMs make, the dictionary team {{26}}. He said that this may be because “it’s so easy to anthropomorphise these systems, treating them as if they had minds of their own.”","The dictionary provides two example sentences of “hallucinate” relating to AI: “LLMs are notorious for hallucinating – {{27}}, often supported by fictitious citations” and “The latest version of the chatbot is greatly improved but it will still hallucinate facts.”","It wasn’t only Cambridge’s decision {{28}}: according to Collins dictionary, the word of 2023 is “AI” itself."],"bank":[{"key":"A","text":"to interact with safely and effectively"},{"key":"B","text":"generating completely false answers"},{"key":"C","text":"a verb that gained an additional meaning this year"},{"key":"D","text":"having taken a drug"},{"key":"E","text":"decided on a “vivid psychological verb”"},{"key":"F","text":"came as a surprise to online dictionary users"},{"key":"G","text":"produces false information"},{"key":"H","text":"to bring their critical thinking skills"},{"key":"I","text":"proves to be correct based on research"},{"key":"K","text":"to pick a word connected to AI"},{"key":"L","text":"to create the accurate and up-to-date information"}],"items":[{"n":0,"example":true,"answer":"C"},{"n":21},{"n":22},{"n":23},{"n":24},{"n":25},{"n":26},{"n":27},{"n":28}]}$mtr$::jsonb, null)
